  "content": {
    "id": "p16-q3-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "Consider the quadratic equation with integer coefficients $ax^2+bx+c=0$.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p16-q3a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Explain why, if $\\Delta=0$, the roots are rational numbers.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The repeated root $x=-\\frac{b}{2a}$ is rational because it is a quotient of integers with a non-zero denominator.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}x&=\\frac{-b\\pm\\sqrt{0}}{2a}\\\\x&=-\\frac{b}{2a}\\end{align*}$$\nSince $a$ and $b$ are integers and $a\\ne0$, the repeated root is rational.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q3b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "What can we say about the roots if $\\Delta$ is a perfect square?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The roots are rational. There are two distinct roots for a positive perfect square and one repeated root for zero.",
          "worked": "If $\\Delta$ is a perfect square, $\\sqrt{\\Delta}$ is an integer. In $x=\\frac{-b\\pm\\sqrt{\\Delta}}{2a}$, each numerator and the non-zero denominator are integers. A positive square gives two distinct rational roots. Zero gives one repeated rational root.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 12
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q3c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "What can we say about the roots if $\\Delta>0$ but is not a perfect square?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Two distinct irrational roots.",
          "worked": "A positive integer that is not a perfect square has a positive irrational square root. The two numerators ${-b\\pm\\sqrt{\\Delta}}$ are distinct and irrational. Dividing by the non-zero integer $2a$ keeps both roots irrational.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      }
    ]
  },
